## Anti-Patterns to Watch For

| Anti-pattern | What happens | Correct behavior |
|---|---|---|
| **Loop echo** | "Let me verify X" → reads X → "Let me double-check X" → reads X again | Read once, trust the result |
| **Phantom execution** | Reasons about running lint, then skips the actual call | Every planned tool call must be emitted |
| **Tool invention** | Calls `findInFiles()` when only `Grep` exists | Use only defined tool names |
| **Serial drip-feeding** | Reads 1 file per turn for 10 turns | Read all needed files in 1 turn |
| **Context bloat** | Reads entire 500-line file when 10 lines are relevant | Read with line ranges or grep |
| **False verification** | "The code looks correct" without running typecheck/lint | Run actual verification commands |
| **Phantom assumptions** | Cites files as data without verifying they exist | Glob/Shell check before citing referenced files |
| **Fragmented debug** | Dispatches 3 subagents for one bug instead of 1 comprehensive | One subagent with all hypotheses |
| **Blind subagent trust** | Presents subagent diagnosis as fact without verification | Independently verify at least one key claim |
| **Stale API knowledge** | Recommends API changes based on training data | WebSearch/WebFetch current docs first |
| **Nested subagents** | Level 2+ spawns children, falls back to older model | Flatten to level 1, dispatch from parent |

---

## Enforcement Priority

These guardrails override general behavioral tendencies:

1. **Never hallucinate tools** — correctness > convenience
2. **Never skip tool calls you planned** — action > reasoning
3. **Never repeat verified checks** — progress > re-verification
4. **Always batch independent operations** — efficiency > caution
5. **Never claim results without evidence** — truth > confidence
6. **Escalate after 2 empty results** — strategy switch > retry
7. **One subagent per bug** — comprehensive dispatch > fragmented narrow scopes
8. **Research before API changes** — current docs > training memory
9. **No nested subagents** — flatten to level 1 > unreliable level 2+
